book_keeper.log -- vco_lc (cross-coupled NMOS LC VCO, sky130A)
BUDGET N = 15 iterations, from example/test_vco/design_constraints.json
           (schematic_sizing_iterations; caller did NOT override -- first pass)
TARGET IN FORCE : sizing/harder_target_spec.json (aim) scored ALONGSIDE
                  spec/target_spec.json (the requirement) on every iteration.
MEASUREMENT     : sizing/run_sizing_vco_lc.py -> the filed deck run verbatim
                  against vco_lc_tuning.sp -> vco_lc_pre_results.txt ->
                  process_vco_lc_results.py. Every number below is ngspice's.
OP-POINT DATA   : NONE, ALL ITERATIONS. generate_op_probe.py is the only
                  producer and it cannot splice this .control-native oscillator
                  deck (3 hard failures, see run_sizing_vco_lc.py's docstring).
                  Reasoning therefore uses spec deltas + the analytic tank model
                  in spec_analysis.log, which reconciles with measurement:
                  C_node = 856 fF back-solved from f0 vs ~856 fF summed
                  device-by-device from the netlist.
TUNABLES        : N0_W/N0_L (XN0,XN1) . N2_W/N2_L (XN2,XN3) . N5_W/N5_L
                  (XN4,XN5) . N4_M (XN4 branch) . C0_W/C0_L . C1_W/C1_L
                  XL0/XL1 + all of vco_spiral_ind: NOT TUNED (no w/l exists)
================================================================================

--- ITER 0 -- baseline re-measure through the tuning netlist -------------------
Changed   nothing tunable. BUT setup_sizing.py's mirror-family normalization had
          already rewritten XN4 from `w=60 nf=1 m=1` to `w=15 nf=1 m=4`
          (shared N5_W=15 with the reference, ratio moved into the branch M).
          Total width 60 um and the topology are preserved. This is the one `m`
          edit the skill permits, and it RESOLVED circuit_decomposition.yaml's
          TG2 ratio_conflict -- the 4:1 now lives in m, where it is tunable.
Specs     OscFreq 2.72238 GHz | TuningRange 227.41 MHz | OutputPower 8.04522 dBm | Power 2.72244 mW
Target    real: 3 MISS (OscFreq, TuningRange, OutputPower), 1 MET (Power). harder: same.
Op-point  none available.
Reasoning Ran BEFORE changing anything, to test whether the m-refactor moved the
          operating point. The skill warns it does; this measures how much
          instead of assuming.
Result    IT DID, and materially. Against the golden baseline (2.72465 / 226.43 /
          8.66583 / 2.87729):
            Power       2.877 -> 2.722 mW   (-5.4%)  margin over the 3.0 ceiling
                                                     widens 4.1% -> 9.3%
            OutputPower 8.666 -> 8.045 dBm  (-0.62 dB)
            OscFreq/TuningRange essentially unchanged (< 0.1%)
          Four 15 um copies land on a different model bin than one 60 um device
          and draw ~5% less current. NET FAVOURABLE: it gives back headroom on
          the binding key (Power) and costs amplitude, which iter 1 was about to
          gain a great deal of anyway.
Next      This, not the golden baseline, is the true starting point. Attack the
          primary knob.

--- ITER 1 -- shrink the MiM plates (the primary knob) --------------------------
Changed   C0_W 16 -> 9.5, C0_L 16 -> 9.5, C1_W 16 -> 9.5, C1_L 16 -> 9.5
          (set together by hand: setup_sizing.py did NOT group XC0 with XC1
          although circuit_decomposition.yaml's TG3 requires them tied. The
          runner's ENFORCED_TIES re-imposes it and refuses+rolls back a
          desynchronised write.)
Specs     OscFreq 2.72238 -> 3.54445 GHz  (+0.82207)
          TuningRange 227.41 -> 451.31 MHz  (+223.90)
          OutputPower 8.04522 -> 12.6614 dBm  (+4.616)
          Power 2.72244 -> 2.73174 mW  (+0.0093)
Target    real: OscFreq MET, OutputPower MET, Power MET, TuningRange MISS (451 vs 600).
Op-point  none available.
Reasoning spec_analysis.log's C budget: C_node must fall from ~856 fF to ~519 fF
          for 3.5 GHz, and the MiM pair alone holds ~524 fF of it. Solving
          2.0*a^2 + 0.19*4a = 187 fF gives a = 9.55 um; used 9.5.
          PREDICTED: f0 ~3.5 GHz; TuningRange up (the varactor's FRACTION of
          C_tank rises even though the varactor itself is untouched);
          OutputPower up ~+2.2 dB (R_p,coil = (w0*L)^2/Rs scales as w0^2, x1.65
          for 2.72 -> 3.5 GHz, at zero extra current); Power flat.
Result    Every direction predicted correctly. f0 landed at 3.544 vs 3.5
          predicted -- the tank model is good to ~1.3%. TWO PREDICTIONS BEAT:
          OutputPower gained +4.62 dB against +2.17 predicted, so the free lunch
          is larger than modelled (the non-coil tank losses fall with frequency
          too, rather than staying fixed as the conservative model assumed).
          TuningRange nearly doubled from a knob that never touched the varactor
          -- spec_analysis.log's central claim, that OscFreq and TuningRange are
          ALIGNED on this knob rather than opposed, is confirmed by measurement.
Next      Only TuningRange short, by 149 MHz. Spend the secondary knob and pay
          for it with the primary one.

--- ITER 2 -- widen the varactor, shrink the MiM to hold f0 (CONVERGED) --------
Changed   N2_W 40 -> 60  (XN2, XN3 together via the TG4 group)
          C0_W/C0_L/C1_W/C1_L 9.5 -> 7.5
Specs     OscFreq 3.54445 -> 3.56407 GHz  (+0.01962)
          TuningRange 451.31 -> 703.98 MHz  (+252.67)
          OutputPower 12.6614 -> 12.6558 dBm  (-0.0056)
          Power 2.73174 -> 2.73107 mW  (-0.0007)
Target    real:   ALL FOUR MET.
          harder: ALL FOUR MET  -> CONVERGED (stop condition 1).
Op-point  none available.
Reasoning Two knobs against two keys. Holding f0 at 3.5 GHz fixes C_node at
          ~519 fF; of that the coil takes 146 fF and the core ~6, so
          MiM = 367 - C_var. At varactor w=60 (C_var ~249 fF) that is MiM ~118
          fF -> plate ~7.5 um. Predicted TuningRange from
          TR/f0 = (1/2)*dC_var/C_tank with dC_var scaled 60/40 from iter 1's
          measured 129 fF: ~652 MHz.
          Step 2a fold check run first (dry): every width inside the PDK max
          (N0_W 20, N2_W 40->60, N5_W 15 vs wmax 100), nothing to fold.
Result    TuningRange 704 MHz vs 652 predicted -- BEAT the prediction by 8%,
          because the varactor's dC scales slightly faster than its width.
          OutputPower did NOT drop: 12.6614 -> 12.6558 dBm, i.e. -0.006 dB
          across a 50% varactor widening. THIS IS AN IMPORTANT NEGATIVE RESULT:
          spec_analysis.log's tension T3 (varactor width degrades tank Q and so
          amplitude) is REAL IN PRINCIPLE BUT NEGLIGIBLE HERE. It also means
          there is no Q lever available to buy amplitude back later.
Next      Converged. Budget remains (11 of 15). The remaining question is not a
          spec but the agent's objective: lowest power among passing netlists.

--- ITER 3 -- power-minimization probe: mirror ratio 4:1 -> 3:1 -----------------
Changed   N4_M 4 -> 3   (XN4's branch multiplier; the ONLY device knob on Power)
Specs     OscFreq 3.56407 -> 3.55507 GHz  (-0.00900)
          TuningRange 703.98 -> 759.54 MHz  (+55.56)
          OutputPower 12.6558 -> 10.3123 dBm  (-2.344)
          Power 2.73107 -> 2.18784 mW  (-0.54323, -19.9%)
Target    real:   ALL FOUR MET.
          harder: OutputPower MISS (10.312 vs 11.0). Other three MET.
Op-point  none available.
Reasoning Not required by any spec -- all four keys already passed at iter 2.
          Run because schematic-agent's objective is the LOWEST-POWER netlist
          that meets every key, and Power's only device knob is this integer
          mirror ratio. PREDICTED: amplitude falls with I_tail in the
          current-limited regime, A1 ~ (4/pi)*I*R_tank, so 4->3 costs
          20*log10(4/3) = 2.50 dB, landing OutputPower at ~10.16 dBm.
Result    Measured -2.344 dB against -2.50 predicted: amplitude IS essentially
          proportional to I_tail, confirming the circuit runs current-limited
          and NOT headroom-clipped. Power fell 19.9%. TuningRange rose 56 MHz
          (a smaller swing loads the varactor over a more favourable arc).
          BUT OutputPower margin over the REAL floor collapses from 2.66 dB to
          0.31 dB, and the harder bar is missed.
Next      Is there a netlist between these two? NO, and this is settled rather
          than assumed: (a) M is an integer count of parallel devices, so
          {3, 4} are adjacent -- nothing lies between; (b) N5_W scales the
          reference AND the leg together, so it moves Vdsat/headroom, not the
          4:1 ratio, and is not a current knob; (c) the Q lever that could have
          bought amplitude back was measured dead at iter 2 (-0.006 dB across a
          50% varactor change). So the choice is exactly these two netlists.
          DECISION: revert to M=4 and ship the CONVERGED one. The harder bar is
          this project's encoded allowance for parasitics that do not exist
          yet, and circuit_decomposition.yaml ranks the tank nodes `severity:
          high` with a mechanism that attacks OutputPower directly (added
          layout C lowers f0, R_p falls as w0^2, amplitude falls with it).
          0.31 dB will not survive that. M=3 is REPORTED TO THE USER as a
          measured 19.9%-lower-power alternative, not silently discarded --
          spending margin is the user's call, not the sizer's.

